format 1
adapter curriculum 1
unit: radioactivity

objective u2-natural-sources
statement: Name natural places radioactivity and radiation come from: rocks and soil, radon in air, food and the body, space.
misconception: Radioactivity is something humans created, and it only exists in nuclear facilities.

item u2-l5-choice-radon
type: choice
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-background-radiation

prompt:
    Where does radon come from?

options:
    {"id":"ground","label":"From radioactive materials in the ground"}
    {"id":"plants","label":"Only from nuclear power plants","note":"Radon is made naturally in the ground. It is in the air everywhere."}
    {"id":"people","label":"From a gas that people make and sell","note":"Radon is natural. It comes from decay in rocks and soil."}

answer: ground

explanation:
    Radioactive materials in the ground, such as uranium and thorium, produce radon. It is natural.

item u2-l5-choice-space
type: choice
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-background-radiation

prompt:
    Which of these arrives on Earth from space?

options:
    {"id":"cosmic","label":"Cosmic radiation"}
    {"id":"radon","label":"Radon gas","note":"Radon comes from the ground."}
    {"id":"k40","label":"Potassium-40 in food","note":"Potassium-40 is in food and in the body."}

answer: cosmic

explanation:
    Cosmic radiation arrives from space all the time.

item u2-l5-order-first
type: order
difficulty: 2
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-becquerel-1896
references: ref-iaea-decay

prompt:
    Put these in order, from first to last.

items:
    {"id":"nature","label":"Radioactive materials are in the ground"}
    {"id":"found","label":"Becquerel notices a fogged photographic plate"}
    {"id":"made","label":"People learn to make radioactive materials"}

explanation:
    Radioactivity was in nature first. Becquerel found it in 1896, and only later did people learn to make radioactive materials.

item u2-l5-predict-where
type: predict
surfaces: lesson

prompt:
    Which of these can have radioactive material in it?

input: choice

options:
    {"id":"plant","label":"A nuclear power plant"}
    {"id":"hospital","label":"A hospital"}
    {"id":"rocks","label":"Rocks and food"}
    {"id":"body","label":"Your own body"}
    {"id":"all","label":"All of these"}

answer: all

explanation:
    Your guess is not scored. The answer comes later in the lesson.

item u2-l5-sort-world
type: sort
difficulty: 2
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-background-radiation

prompt:
    Which kind is it? Natural radioactivity, cosmic radiation, or made by people? Sort each one.

buckets:
    {"id":"natural","label":"Natural radioactivity","icon":"AqMountain"}
    {"id":"cosmic","label":"Cosmic radiation","icon":"AqCosmic"}
    {"id":"people","label":"Made by people","icon":"AqFlask"}

items:
    {"id":"banana","label":"A banana","bucket":"natural"}
    {"id":"rock","label":"A rock","bucket":"natural"}
    {"id":"body","label":"The human body","bucket":"natural"}
    {"id":"plane","label":"An airplane at high altitude","bucket":"cosmic"}
    {"id":"reactor","label":"A nuclear reactor","bucket":"people"}
    {"id":"hospital","label":"A hospital radiation source","bucket":"people"}

explanation:
    A banana, a rock and your body hold natural radioactivity. High up, an airplane gets more cosmic radiation. Reactors and hospital sources are made by people. Radiation is part of the world, not only inside nuclear facilities.

item u2-l5-tom-facilities
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: lesson
surfaces: practice
surfaces: review
references: ref-background-radiation

statement:
    Radioactive materials are only found in nuclear facilities.

answer: myth

explanation:
    Myth. Radioactive materials are in rocks, soil, air, food and your own body. Nuclear facilities are only one place.

item u2-x-natural-source
type: choice
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-background-radiation

prompt:
    Which of these is a natural source of radioactivity?

options:
    {"id":"k40","label":"Potassium-40 in food"}
    {"id":"reactor","label":"A radioactive source made in a reactor","note":"That one is made by people."}
    {"id":"nowhere","label":"Nothing: it only comes from nuclear plants","note":"Radioactivity is in rocks, air, food, bodies and space."}

answer: k40

explanation:
    Potassium-40 is a natural radioactive material found in food and in the body.

item u2-x-tom-radon
type: true_or_myth
surfaces: practice
surfaces: checkpoint
surfaces: review
references: ref-background-radiation

statement:
    Radon is a radioactive gas that comes from the ground.

answer: true

explanation:
    True. Radioactive materials in the ground produce radon, a natural radioactive gas.
